    "---\n",
    "## 1 - Setup and Imports\n",
    "\n",
    "Before writing any machine learning code we need to install and import the right tools. Think of this section like unpacking your toolbox before starting a project.\n",
    "\n",
    "**What each library does in plain English:**\n",
    "\n",
    "- **torch / torchvision** - The core deep learning engine. It does the maths of training neural networks, runs them on the GPU, and handles tensors (the multi-dimensional arrays that store image data).\n",
    "- **timm** - A library full of pretrained neural networks ready to use. Instead of training from scratch (which takes weeks), we download a network already trained on millions of photos and fine-tune it on our circle images.\n",
    "- **albumentations** - A fast image augmentation library. Augmentation means randomly modifying training images (flipping, rotating, adjusting brightness) so the model sees more variety and doesn't memorise the training set.\n",
    "- **pandas / numpy** - Standard tools for loading CSV files and doing array maths.\n",
    "- **sklearn** — Provides the stratified train/validation split and evaluation metrics.\n",
    "- **matplotlib / seaborn** - For plotting charts and visualising results.\n",
    "- **PIL** - The Python Imaging Library, used to open and inspect image files.\n",
    "- **tqdm** - Prints a live progress bar while the model trains.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why do we fix random seeds?**\n",
    "\n",
    "Neural networks start with random weights, and data loading involves random shuffling. If we set the same seed (42) everywhere before running the code, those random choices are always the same. This means the experiment is reproducible: running the notebook twice gives the same result."
   ]
  },
